{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 14409 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "23d49d550cc53d278d2f48114ab082009d701b735f3d946bb80887460764bc7e" :edb/db-before-t 14408 :edb/db-after-t 14409 :edb/tx-data [[13194139547721 50 #inst "2026-11-13T16:41:49.753Z" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1117 #edb/ref 17592186761936 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186048157 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186048593 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186049668 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186051060 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761906 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761915 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761906 1062 #uuid "0483464d-afc1-59ca-a243-a7d894a83946" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761906 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761943 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761907 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761955 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761907 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761907 1077 "answer" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761907 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761955 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761907 1079 #uuid "09d9e7ad-81d9-5f7d-a366-2534e1078648" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761908 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761953 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761908 1062 #uuid "0d124e95-43ee-5e2c-9fae-45f0a38b379d" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761908 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761929 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761909 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761911 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761909 1062 #uuid "0e5d9fdf-5726-5e35-9b03-7efa9ba91c9c" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761909 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761919 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761910 1064 "**MTH 256 — Recitation 7: Quiz 3 Review**\n\nDue: unknown\nDate imported: 2026-11-13\nSource: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review.pdf)\nAnswer key: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review Key.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review%20Key.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, independently checked against the downloaded key. Problems 1 and 10 contain source inconsistencies; their original wording is preserved and the verified conclusions are explained in the answer reveals. The instructor's intended corrections remain unconfirmed. Neither PDF specifies a due date.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThe recitation this week serves as a review for quiz three, which will be taken in the latter half of recitation time. It also prepares you for the wording on the quiz. The quiz covers Sections 5.1 and 5.2 and Euler equations from Section 7.4. The following definition and theorems will be provided on the quiz (source PDF p. 1).\n\n### Wronskian definition\n\nFor two functions $\\{y_1(x),y_2(x)\\}$,\n$$W(x)=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2.$$\n\n**Topics:** [Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.1.%20Theory%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Linear%20ODEs/Lessons/3.1.6.%20Linear%20Independence%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Homogeneous%20ODEs.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 5.2.1 — Constant coefficients\n\nLet $p(r)=ar^2+br+c$ be the characteristic polynomial of\n$$ay''+by'+cy=0.\\tag{5.2.20}$$\n\n(a) If $p(r)=0$ has distinct real roots $r_1,r_2$, then the general solution is\n$$y=c_1e^{r_1x}+c_2e^{r_2x}.$$\n\n(b) If it has a repeated root $r_1$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{r_1x}(c_1+c_2x).$$\n\n(c) If it has complex conjugate roots $r_1=\\lambda+i\\omega$ and $r_2=\\lambda-i\\omega$, where $\\omega>0$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{\\lambda x}(c_1\\cos\\omega x+c_2\\sin\\omega x).$$\n\n**Topics:** [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.1.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.2.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.3.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 7.4.3 — Euler equations\n\nSuppose the roots of the indicial equation\n$$ar(r-1)+br+c=0\\tag{7.4.11}$$\nare $r_1,r_2$. Then the general solution of the Euler equation\n$$ax^2y''+bxy'+cy=0\\tag{7.4.12}$$\non $(0,\\infty)$ is\n\n- $y=c_1x^{r_1}+c_2x^{r_2}$ if $r_1,r_2$ are distinct real numbers;\n- $y=x^{r_1}(c_1+c_2\\ln x)$ if $r_1=r_2$;\n- $y=x^\\lambda[c_1\\cos(\\omega\\ln x)+c_2\\sin(\\omega\\ln x)]$ if $r_1,r_2=\\lambda\\pm i\\omega$ with $\\omega>0$.\n\n**Topics:** [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.1.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.2.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.3.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761910 1066 #uuid "0f97993b-9e3a-5621-807f-4cb2646d6ab5" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761910 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761911 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761940 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761911 1062 #uuid "0ff64869-4264-5def-81ec-54f44f853149" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761911 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761921 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761956 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761912 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 5, Problem 9.\n\nThe indicial polynomial for\n$$2x^2y''-3xy'+y=0$$\nis $p(r)=2r(r-1)-3r+1$. Find the solutions $r$ to the indicial equation $p(r)=0$.\n$r=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761912 1072 #uuid "13cc7856-c9e2-5862-a4fb-c5632cf22327" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761912 1074 "Expand first: $2r(r-1)-3r+1=2r^2-5r+1$. The quadratic formula gives $r=(5\\pm\\sqrt{25-8})/4=(5\\pm\\sqrt{17})/4$. Their sum is $5/2$ and product is $1/2$, agreeing with the coefficients of the quadratic." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761914 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761913 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 2, Problem 2.\n\nThe set of functions $\\{e^{3x},xe^{3x}\\}$ solve\n$$y''-6y'+9y=0.$$\nShow that $\\{e^{3x},xe^{3x}\\}$ form a fundamental set of solutions for $0<x<\\infty$. Your solution must include a Wronskian calculation." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761913 1072 #uuid "18d87466-3553-57d8-a0e9-964f47e356ae" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761913 1074 "Apply the product rule to $xe^{3x}$; its derivative contains both $e^{3x}$ and $3xe^{3x}$. The $3xe^{6x}$ terms then cancel in the Wronskian." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761914 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761947 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761914 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761914 1077 "response" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761914 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761947 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761914 1079 #uuid "1a978cf8-fde7-5977-b5dd-b989254a621b" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761915 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761925 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761915 1062 #uuid "1abe3230-4dea-554c-8f05-5776dcde1a1b" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761915 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761952 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761916 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761909 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761916 1062 #uuid "20d15ec8-9721-55e4-a249-ad92e3654daa" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761916 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761945 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761923 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761917 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 5, Problem 10.\n\nThe source states that the indicial equation\n$$r^2+2r-3=0$$\nfor the Euler equation\n$$y''+3xy'-3y=0$$\nhas solutions $r_1=-1$ and $r_2=3$. Find the general solution to\n$$x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0.$$\n\n**Source conflict:** The first displayed ODE omits the $x^2$ multiplying $y''$, and the stated roots do not solve the printed indicial equation. For the study answer, solve the final displayed ODE on $(0,\\infty)$, using the Euler-theorem interval from page 1.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761917 1072 #uuid "228f22c4-9d80-5d95-bd19-73b3f5bad258" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761917 1074 "Substitute $y=x^r$ into the final ODE to get $r(r-1)+3r-3=r^2+2r-3=(r-1)(r+3)=0$. Its actual roots are $1$ and $-3$, so $y=C_1x+C_2x^{-3}$ on $(0,\\infty)$. Each term has residual zero in the final ODE.\n\nThe key follows the erroneous stated roots and gives $C_1x^{-1}+C_2x^3$. These terms have residuals $-4x^{-1}$ and $12x^3$, respectively, so that expression is not the general solution of the final printed equation. It would instead solve $x^2y''-xy'-3y=0$. The original wording is retained above; no instructor correction has been assumed." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761918 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761957 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761918 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761918 1077 "answer" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761918 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761957 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761918 1079 #uuid "24cb2bff-7b34-5c8b-a442-93558a259094" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761919 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048774 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761919 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049668 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761919 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761938 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761919 1090 #uuid "24ef5537-a7c5-5ef5-830c-e2917d58ab11" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761920 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761934 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761920 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761920 1077 "answer" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761920 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761934 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761920 1079 #uuid "2673b996-840f-5b02-be16-eecc2f38da86" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761921 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048157 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761921 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761926 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761921 1090 #uuid "28731d4f-caef-5346-a863-15015d49c54b" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761922 1062 #uuid "296a779b-ff39-5f0d-8278-3ca5c3910e3a" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761922 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761944 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761923 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761950 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761923 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761923 1077 "answer" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761923 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761950 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761923 1079 #uuid "2a6be089-872c-5fc5-8863-8711b543e132" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761933 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761924 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 4.\n\nFind the characteristic polynomial of the homogeneous second-order linear equation with constant coefficients\n$$-3y''+7y=0.$$\n$p(r)=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761924 1072 #uuid "32211eb5-452e-50c2-87eb-6641cda913b9" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761924 1074 "The coefficients of $y''$, $y'$, and $y$ are $-3$, $0$, and $7$. Thus $p(r)=-3r^2+0r+7=-3r^2+7$. The absence of a $y'$ term gives a zero linear coefficient." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761925 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761916 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761925 1062 #uuid "349f0ba1-7ded-5461-859f-e3003a382d3f" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761925 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761935 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761920 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761926 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 6.\n\nThe homogeneous second-order linear equation $y''-9y=0$ has characteristic polynomial $p(r)=r^2-9$. Find the solution(s) to $p(r)=0$.\n$r=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761926 1072 #uuid "370002b3-18c2-59d0-9262-89109225fc9c" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761926 1074 "Factor $r^2-9=(r-3)(r+3)$; either factor may be zero. The roots are $3$ and $-3$, written $\\pm3$. Both square to $9$." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761946 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761927 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 2, Problem 1.\n\nThe set of functions $\\{x^{-3},x\\}$ solve\n$$x^2y''+4xy'-y=0.$$\nShow that $\\{x^{-3},x\\}$ form a fundamental set of solutions for $0<x<\\infty$. Your solution must include a Wronskian calculation.\n\n**Source conflict:** The worksheet and key print different equations here. The assertion is inconsistent with both printed versions. Preserve the requested Wronskian work and explain what the calculation actually establishes." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761927 1072 #uuid "3b51ca7a-7572-534e-b8b8-82d1a8af6ccb" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761927 1074 "A nonzero Wronskian proves independence; membership in the ODE's solution set must also hold. The key's Wronskian is correct, but it does not establish the claimed solution property. The instructor's intended correction remains unconfirmed." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761928 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761931 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761928 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761928 1077 "answer" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761928 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761931 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761928 1079 #uuid "46ee357d-6deb-55bb-9203-ca8e853c0a9d" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761929 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048289 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761929 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761942 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761929 1090 #uuid "47de9ed3-cbf2-5c15-b450-fec8ce9491e6" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761930 1080 "C_1\\cos(6x)+C_2\\sin(6x)" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761930 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761930 1083 #uuid "515a31fb-6b92-5b6e-99cc-795571b5f37f" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761931 1080 "8r^2-7r+5" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761931 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761931 1083 #uuid "70969c63-a286-5eba-b680-97393e34db71" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761907 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761932 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 4, Problem 7.\n\nThe characteristic equation $r^2-4r+4=0$ for $y''-4y'+4y=0$ has a single real solution $r=2$. Find the general solution to\n$$y''-4y'+4y=0.$$\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761932 1072 #uuid "7523b767-f708-50e1-8742-fd8935f8683a" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761932 1074 "The root $r=2$ is repeated. Two independent solutions are $e^{2x}$ and $xe^{2x}$, so $y=(C_1+C_2x)e^{2x}$, with arbitrary constants. Their Wronskian is $e^{4x}\\ne0$, and each has residual zero in $y''-4y'+4y$. Repeating $e^{2x}$ without the factor $x$ would provide only one independent solution." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761933 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761949 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761933 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761933 1077 "answer" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761933 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761949 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761933 1079 #uuid "8865409b-88f0-570a-95f6-4c61d06a183a" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761934 1080 "\\pm3" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761934 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761934 1083 #uuid "91b2c46b-2c16-5cc2-8f7c-e9becf6bf9c2" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761935 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048391 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761935 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761937 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761935 1090 #uuid "9822610e-970c-5c38-9851-362b00682d6f" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761906 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761908 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761909 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761911 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761915 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761916 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761922 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761925 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761940 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761951 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1043 #edb/ref 17592186761953 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045461 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1045 "MTH 256 — R-7: Quiz 3 Review" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1046 #edb/ref 17592186761951 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1048 #edb/ref 17592186405205 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761936 1053 #uuid "a32d2e13-e205-5c5d-8660-a11bad449a8c" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761928 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761937 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 3.\n\nFind the characteristic polynomial of the homogeneous second-order linear equation with constant coefficients\n$$8y''-7y'+5y=0.$$\n$p(r)=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761937 1072 #uuid "b047f39b-2ef1-5e24-bf80-8e6219b98d6e" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761937 1074 "Substituting $y=e^{rx}$ yields $(8r^2-7r+5)e^{rx}=0$. The characteristic polynomial is $p(r)=8r^2-7r+5$; keep the coefficients and signs from the ODE." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761918 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761938 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 5.\n\nThe homogeneous second-order linear equation $y''-4y'+4y=0$ has characteristic polynomial $p(r)=r^2-4r+4$. Find the solution(s) to $p(r)=0$.\n$r=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761938 1072 #uuid "b4c3214b-c497-5260-8ba7-b9f1e86564ab" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761938 1074 "Factor $r^2-4r+4=(r-2)^2$. Therefore $r=2$ is the only distinct root, with multiplicity two. Substitution gives $4-8+4=0$." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761939 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050465 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761939 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761912 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761939 1090 #uuid "b9747d4f-b1f8-5ecc-92ff-cf1b790d3d2e" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761940 1058 #edb/ref 17592186761908 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761940 1062 #uuid "bae418df-3fb7-58ac-90b7-b6636ae4a95a" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761940 1063 #edb/ref 17592186761941 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761941 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050433 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761941 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761932 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761941 1090 #uuid "bb99a326-7ec9-5b3d-8bdf-c01d5c9d4e15" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761954 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761942 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 4, Problem 8.\n\nThe characteristic equation $r^2+36=0$ for $y''+36y=0$ has solutions $r=\\pm6i$. Find the general solution to\n$$y''+36y=0.$$\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761942 1072 #uuid "bf0095af-d1b1-55db-80a5-342a259f7a9a" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761942 1074 "For roots $\\lambda\\pm i\\omega$, the real solution is $e^{\\lambda x}(C_1\\cos(\\omega x)+C_2\\sin(\\omega x))$. Here $\\lambda=0$ and $\\omega=6$, so the exponential factor is $1$. Twice differentiating either trigonometric term multiplies it by $-36$, which verifies the ODE." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761943 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048593 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761943 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049486 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761943 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050150 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761943 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761927 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761943 1090 #uuid "c0e81fc6-05cc-5f8f-b2dd-6c42bf502c0f" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761944 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048774 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761944 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049486 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761944 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050557 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761944 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761917 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761944 1090 #uuid "c577c933-1af3-50d5-b98b-0fbf19fbf0a6" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761945 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048391 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761945 1089 #edb/ref 17592186761924 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761945 1090 #uuid "ca0652be-5d3b-594f-bd74-e189473df881" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761946 1075 #edb/ref 17592186761948 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761946 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761946 1077 "response" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761946 1078 #edb/ref 17592186761948 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761946 1079 #uuid "cd0ff44b-2295-53ff-a2de-b5a8b8c914e1" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761947 1080 "For $y_1=e^{3x}$ and $y_2=xe^{3x}$,\n$$y_1'=3e^{3x},\\qquad y_2'=(1+3x)e^{3x}.$$\nHence\n$$W=e^{3x}(1+3x)e^{3x}-(3e^{3x})(xe^{3x})=e^{6x}>0.$$\nBoth functions solve the ODE: $y_1''=9e^{3x}$ gives $(9-18+9)e^{3x}=0$, and $y_2''=(6+9x)e^{3x}$ gives $[(6+9x)-6(1+3x)+9x]e^{3x}=0$.\nThe coefficients are continuous on $(0,\\infty)$, and the two solutions have nonzero Wronskian there. They therefore form a fundamental set on the required interval." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761947 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761947 1083 #uuid "cf2a4057-639d-57a9-9518-d7e6dbcb1a17" 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761948 1080 "The requested conclusion is false for the printed ODE. With $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$, the derivatives are $y_1'=-3x^{-4}$ and $y_2'=1$, so\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThus the functions are linearly independent. But for $L[y]=x^2y''+4xy'-y$,\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-12x^{-3}-x^{-3}=-x^{-3},\\qquad L[x]=4x-x=3x.$$\nNeither function solves the printed equation, so they cannot be its fundamental set.\n\nThe key instead prints $x^2y''+3xy'-y=0$; its residuals are $2x^{-3}$ and $2x$, so that version also fails. The equation $x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0$ does have these two solutions, and the same nonzero Wronskian proves they form a fundamental set on $(0,\\infty)$. This last equation is an authored possible correction, not a confirmed instructor correction." 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761948 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139547721 17592186502323 true] [17592186761948 1083 #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" 13194139547721 1759218650232